    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay focuses on the case of President Barack Obama’s public declaration in 2012 that the use of chemical weapons in Syria would amount to crossing an inviolable “red line” and bring about a swift intervention. Kurland points out the potential risks and pitfalls of this “red line” or ultimatum rhetoric, and argues that we can learn much about the potency of presidential rhetoric in delimiting the actions of combatants in conflict zones and in achieving consensus for response.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Kurland, S. (2024). \"Red Lines\" and the Syrian Civil War: The Power of a President. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/sq940fg1316. https://doi.org/10.25740/sq940fg1316.",
